  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 144 PM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nTonight: Line of Showers Moves Through\n\nA narrow line of elevated showers and possibly a few \nthunderstorms are progged to develop ahead of an approaching \ncold front within a pocket of narrow mid-level theta-e \nadvection/30-40-kt LLJ above a stout lower tropospheric warm \nnose. The window of opportunity for storms at a given location \nis short (1-2 hours) and is predicated on the moistening of that\ncritical 800-700-mb layer in time. MUCAPE values of 500-1000 \nJ/kg would certainly support general thunderstorms, though the \nwind fields would limit any organization as indicated by the \ncomplex hodographs. Most of the 11.12Z HREF members have this \nconvection waning by sunrise as it moves towards central \nWisconsin. \n\n\nMonday - Tuesday: Heavy Rain/Severe Weather Risk\n\nThe main period of interest in the forecast remains from Monday\ninto Tuesday as an early fall extratropical cyclone ejects off \nthe northern High Plains and tracks across southern Manitoba and\nOntario. The attendant cold front slides southeastward with \nreturn flow pushing warm sector PWATs close to 2 inches and warm\ncloud depths of 12+ kft as Pacific moisture overruns lower \nlevel Gulf moisture. While the thermodynamic environment will \nsupport efficient rainfall processes, how and where this rain \nfalls will be dictated by the position of the front and \nconvective evolution ahead of the front, which is a source of \nmodel variability between the medium range solutions. Recent \nruns of the EPS/GEFS have started trending towards a more \nprogressive frontal passage, but there still exist a few members\nthat have the front more parallel to the mean upper level flow,\nwhich would be more favorable for repeated rounds of heavy \nrain. There are some solutions that fails to push the front \nsouth of the area on Tuesday and linger the heavy rain potential\ninto Wednesday.\n\nThe risk for severe weather also hinges on how the warm sector \nevolves during the day on Monday and the influences of the \nmorning convection, variables we will not have sorted out until \nlater this weekend.\n\n\nLate Week: Possibly More Storms\n\nLooking beyond early next week, the local weather pattern will \nhinge on the evolution of Pacific Northwest wave Tuesday into \nWednesday. Roughly 20-30% of the current guidance have this wave\npropagating eastward across the region and deepening, resulting\nin the surface baroclinic zone lifting northward into our \nregion and being the focus for renewed rounds of storms and \nheavy rain later in the week. These solutions look to be \nfalling more into the minority and some of the more recent \ndeterministic runs also are favoring this shortwave energy \ncutting off over California and the northern stream energy \nlifting northeastward and not affecting the region. The blended \nNBM forecast understandably has low (20-30%) PoPs for much of \nnext week, but if the cutoff solution unfolds, we may stay dry \nand cool for much of the remainder of the week--or until the \ncutoff low finally ejects eastward.",
